#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e181f272-c259-5dfe-9f39-b3b9e5618d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e181f272-c259-5dfe-9f39-b3b9e5618d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266147} :answer-field/id #uuid "988ea093-d38d-51ab-8d06-7e5be72e5b6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e190c4a3-1ee2-5dcd-b3fa-376f55f2ec5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-314152" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(4+4x){e}^{2x}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+0){e}^{0}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+6}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ffda00-8b89-5130-9819-271e37755096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a003fd-4e3a-50cc-8e19-f882d4516ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066553} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb7ebd7-3376-5bc7-97e2-421e92d39605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} {:answer/id #uuid "155d1ce5-aba5-58d8-b553-12e130aad8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066555} {:answer/id #uuid "40cf65d0-cc70-56c5-94d9-ac421b7f839c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffda00-8b89-5130-9819-271e37755096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552} :answer-field/id #uuid "847a3e94-f49d-52fc-837e-6bc89d115615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71ad4f56-be44-5504-8dcb-a974b170b18f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31597" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2\\ln x}{e^{x}}$?" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440eb8e0-c5a3-524a-9bf3-4130be95649a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266150} {:answer/id #uuid "80a63807-f383-573e-a43b-f82b10839bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80a63807-f383-573e-a43b-f82b10839bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266151} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e7b4d9-3a93-54ee-942e-14e16a7bc102" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266149}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e31f629-06c9-5847-8488-ea8d7bd593df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336897" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-5)-48}{2x-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[2{x}^{3}-10x-24]}^{′}}{{[x-3]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-10}{1} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154} :answer-field/id #uuid "445ecb86-4291-52cd-9f90-72e378f6a2ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1391e51-b3fc-5d6d-af4b-284d914341dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336951" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81bae9ef-c4b4-5c13-99bf-4b41816aea87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068136} {:answer/id #uuid "8648d449-ecb2-50b4-974a-dc968a440298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068137} {:answer/id #uuid "edf2e4f1-2f3b-5c46-adcd-970adbfdf8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068138} {:answer/id #uuid "082cb860-3450-56e7-9b78-0a8f54a657c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068139} {:answer/id #uuid "3804810d-47fe-5289-a1d9-58396bd00d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "082cb860-3450-56e7-9b78-0a8f54a657c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068139} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea85546-6c7b-5400-92cb-9605cfa06823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068135}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9948ce-563f-5b0f-bd31-1937130137d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72339" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0^{+}}\\frac{\\ln (\\sin x)}{\\csc x}$." :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
